Use Aseprite MCP
to draw a **professional, complete** top-down wilderness tileset, replacing the previous chibi grassland.

32x32 tiles, 12-column x 19-row atlas (384x608, 228 slots), transparent background, pure pixel art with no anti-aliasing.
Follow an industry-standard layout: make the transition for each "overlay terrain on top of a base" a **complete 47-tile blob matrix**
(8-neighbour bitmask across 4 edges + 4 corners; a corner bit only matters when both adjacent edges are set,
and the legal corner subsets across the 16 edge combinations add up to exactly 47). Four overlay terrains, one matrix each:
dirt road over grass, sand over grass, water over sand, stone slabs over grass.
Order the atlas by ascending bitmask; slot 48 of each block holds that terrain's interior variant B;
row 16 holds interior variants A/C of the four terrains plus four grass variants; the last two rows' 24 cells hold props.

Materials: **one palette that borrows across ramps** (34 colors); every ramp shifts both value and hue — the dark end
leans blue-purple and the light end leans yellow-orange; a value-only ramp is called a straight ramp and, once laid out,
reads as cheap plastic; dirt/sand/stone share the same cold-purple dark end, and sand and dirt share the same warm beige lit
top face. Light comes consistently from the top-left. Large color fields **must not use value noise** (on a lattice it can
only paint axis-aligned rectangles, so the road turns into a checkerboard); use a distance field of round clumps with
different radii instead; cut the tiers at quantiles of that material's own field, not at hand-tuned thresholds; and pull a
layer of dominant-color edge around color-patch boundaries (a thin transition color). Small-scale detail uses readable 2-3
pixel strokes (grass blades, grit, ripples, brick seams), and is not nailed to fixed columns (the texture has no obvious
directionality). Every terrain gets at least 3 interior variants.

Borders must be ragged: the corner arc radius is 6 (8 turns the pond into flower petals), and the border line itself
drifts ±2px along the edge — but seed the jitter curve by "which edge" it is, not by bitmask, or two adjacent tiles will
each jitter their own way and break at the seam; the two sides of a border must intrude on each other (grass blades poke
into the dirt road, grit spills outside the road edge), with frequencies down at 1/9 and 1/7 — any denser and it is not an
organic border but a dirty one.

The border language must be systematic: the ring of the overlay region that touches the base gets a lit edge (facing up or
left) or a shadow edge (facing down or right); the ring of the base that touches the overlay gets a contact shadow. Water
is the exception — its border is a foam line that translates with the phase, and its horizontal period must divide the tile
width; water depth may only be tiered by distance from shore, and the far tier must be pixel-identical to the interior tile.

The water surface is a 4-frame loop animation (tagged water_loop); only the water tiles and the water interior variants
change with the phase.

Then use these 228 tiles to assemble a 24x16-tile (768x512) example scene: a two-tile-wide diagonal dirt road, a rounded
pond in the bottom-right corner (sand bank + water + lily pads + reeds), two stone-slab piles, trees, bushes, flowers,
mushrooms, a fallen log, fences.
**The scene must not hand-place tiles**: draw a terrain map first, then lay tiles out automatically by blob lookup, so a
missing tile or an unsupported adjacency surfaces on the spot. Higher-priority terrain paints above lower-priority terrain
(water paints over sand), so sand's blob is not cut off by the water beside it. Place props by the rule of which terrains
they can sit on.

Before delivery it must pass three structural checks; "no errors reported" is not a substitute:
1. The 47 bitmasks are unique and complete; the full mask fills the whole tile, and the empty mask is an isolated island that must not touch the tile border by a single pixel;
2. Seam contract: for two same-terrain neighbours, the middle of their shared border must have identical per-pixel coverage;
3. The PNG Aseprite writes and the design matrix differ by 0 pixels, and the 4 water phases are pairwise different.

When done, output: the tileset PNG, a 2x labelled preview (each cell labelled with slot index / block name / bitmask in
hex), the scene PNG (2x), the water loop GIF, two ase files, and a manifest.json (slot index -> row/column / block /
bitmask / variant / animated + palette) for engine import.
